SUMMARY:Other Events: Donovan Rasamoelison\, University of California\, S
 anta Barbara - Screening and Compliance Documentation as Complements? Ef
 fects on Fraud and Administrative Burden
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Donovan Rasamoelison\, University of California\, Sa
 nta Barbara\nTitle: "Screening and Compliance Documentation as Complemen
 ts? Effects on Fraud and Administrative Burden"\n\nThis paper investigat
 es whether compliance documentation offer incremental fraud deterrence b
 eyond initial ex-ante screening in public programs by leveraging a polic
 y feature of the Paycheck Protection Program - a loan-size threshold tha
 t waived compliance documentation requirements for loan forgiveness. On 
 one hand\, the waiver may encourage firms with true loan eligibility bel
 ow the threshold to opportunistically inflate their loan requests up to 
 the threshold. On the other hand\, firms with true loan amounts above th
 e threshold may strategically reduce their loans to avoid the administra
 tive burden of compliance. Quantitatively\, opportunistic overclaiming w
 as limited\, and the loan-reducing effect outweighed the loan-increasing
  effect\, resulting in a net welfare gain of $0.03 per dollar borrowed f
 rom the documentation waiver. More broadly\, when screening is in place\
 , a higher probability of random audit can substitute for universal comp
 liance documentation to limit fraud. Documentation requirements may impo
 se substantial compliance burdens while providing limited marginal fraud
  deterrence beyond the initial screening process. JEL Classification Cod
 es: D04\, D21\, D22\, D60\, H81\, H83\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/al
 l/2026/amel-donovan-rasamoelison-university-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/amel-donovan-rasamoelison-unive
 rsity-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:OTHER
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:32eb24e68c6395c116cc@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261004T171852Z
DTSTART:20261007T223000Z
DTEND:20261008T000000Z
SUMMARY:Other Events: Yihong Liu\, University of California\, Santa Barba
 ra - Matched Triple Differences: A Framework for Covariate Adjustment
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Yihong Liu\, University of California\, Santa Barbar
 a\nTitle: "Matched Triple Differences: A Framework for Covariate Adjustm
 ent"\nHosted by: Daniel Martin\n\nIdentification in triple-differences (
 DDD) designs is often justified by a conditional parallel gaps assumptio
 n\, under which units with similar observed covariates are expected to e
 xhibit similar deviations from parallel trends. In this setting\, the co
 mmon empirical estimation strategy takes one of the two forms: adding th
 e covariates\, or their interactions with time indicators\, to a three-w
 ay fixed effects (3WFE) regression. We formally study both practices and
  show that\, in general\, neither consistently estimates the average tre
 atment effect on the treated (ATT). Our diagnosis has two parts. First\,
  the OLS estimands of both 3WFE specifications fail to satisfy the covar
 iate balancing condition that is sufficient for them to equal the ATT. S
 econd\, we characterize the additional restrictive assumptions for each 
 OLS estimand to equal the ATT. To address this limitation\, we propose a
  \\emph{matched triple-differences} framework that achieves the sufficie
 nt covariate balance for consistent estimation. Within this framework\, 
 we construct a class of consistent matching estimators\, establish their
  asymptotic normality\, and provide a consistent variance estimator acco
 unting for the variability introduced by matching. The framework accommo
 dates a general class of matching procedures\, including nearest-neighbo
 r matching and kernel matching. Our empirical application to \\cite{Cai_
 2016_AEJ} shows that our proposed matching estimators can yield estimate
 s and qualitative conclusions that differ from those obtained from the 3
 WFE regression. Keywords: Triple-Differences\; Difference-in-Differences
 -Differences\; Parallel Gaps\; Matching JEL Classification: C14\, C21\, 
 C23\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-yihong-li
 u-university-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-yihong-liu-un
 iversity-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:OTHER
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
