SUMMARY:Other Events: Juan Nicolas Herrera\, University of California\, S
 anta Barbara - Value-Added Tax Incidence in Housing Markets
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Juan Nicolas Herrera\, University of California\, Sa
 nta Barbara\nTitle: "Value-Added Tax Incidence in Housing Markets"\nHost
 ed by: Daniel Martin\n\nWe study the VAT incidence in the housing market
 \, exploiting a 2016 reform inChile that introduced a 19% tax on sales b
 y developers while exempting resales by in-dividual homeowners. Using ri
 ch administrative data covering nearly the universe ofresidential transa
 ctions\, we estimate a difference-in-differences design that leveragesth
 e differential tax treatment across seller types. Apartment prices per s
 quare meterrose by approximately 11.8% within three years\, with consume
 rs bearing 75% of theeffective tax rate. This increase combines two marg
 ins: an 8.5% rise in nominal pricesand a 3.2% decline in unit sizes\, a 
 form ofhousing shrinkflationvisible in constructionpermits before any pr
 ice response. Despite the large tax\, transaction volumes did notdecline
 . Purchase contracts surged by 33% before implementation and fell by a c
 om-parable amount immediately after\, indicating pure re-timing rather t
 han a permanentdemand response. The tax raised revenue without reducing 
 market activity\, but at thecost of higher prices and smaller apartments
  for buyers. JEL Classification Codes: H22\, H25\, R21\, R31\n\nhttps://
 econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-juan-nicolas-herrera-uni
 versity-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-juan-nicolas-
 herrera-university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:OTHER
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e66c4de8ecc42704a23f@econ.ucsb.edu
DTSTAMP:20261004T171852Z
DTSTART:20261021T223000Z
DTEND:20261022T000000Z
SUMMARY:Other Events: Mauricio Collado\, University of California\, Santa
  Barbara - Quota Baskets Versus Fish Baskets in Fisheries
LOCATION:North Hall 2111
DESCRIPTION:Speaker: Mauricio Collado\, University of California\, Santa 
 Barbara\nTitle: "Quota Baskets Versus Fish Baskets in Fisheries"\nHosted
  by: Daniel Martin\n\nBecause managing stocks individually is costly\, m
 any fisheries bundle multiple stocks and manage them with a single instr
 ument. I study when such bundling is desirable and whether it should tak
 e the form of a shared quota or a shared landing fee\, extending the con
 stant-escapement framework of Reed (1979) and Weitzman (2002) to a multi
 species fishery under ecological uncertainty. The fundamental condition 
 for a well-designed basket is equalizing marginal profits across species
 \, a condition that combines biological productivity\, harvesting costs\
 , and prices. Under this criterion\, I find that the tax basket outperfo
 rms the quota basket. However\, two qualifications refine this result: (
 i) both instruments coincide and are first-best when marginal profits ar
 e equalized at each species' optimal escapement in a deterministic setti
 ng\; and (ii) as the number of species in the group increases\, the quot
 a's uncertainty loss is diversified away\, reducing the average per-spec
 ies welfare gap. Yet increasing the basket size has a second cost: the m
 ismatch from pooling heterogeneous species grows with the number of spec
 ies for both instruments. Larger baskets are not always better. Bundling
  improves on species-specific management only when the monitoring costs 
 it saves exceed this pooling loss. JEL Classification Codes: Q22\, Q28\,
  Q58\, D81\n\nhttps://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-ma
 uricio-collado-university-california-santa-barbara
URL:https://econ.ucsb.edu/events/all/2026/practice-job-talk-mauricio-coll
 ado-university-california-santa-barbara
CATEGORIES:OTHER
SEQUENCE:1
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
